# PART 5 — COMPLETELY IMPROVE ANIMAL LOCOMOTION AND ORIENTATION

This needs significant attention.

The existing animals have good procedural anatomical foundations, but their movement and orientation remain insufficiently natural.

The current models use positive local Z as their forward direction, while important movement and camera calculations currently use the opposite world direction.

This must be corrected systematically.

## 5.1 Establish one canonical forward-direction convention

Audit every animal mesh and the associated movement transformations.

The animal models currently face local +Z.

For the existing shader rotation convention, the corresponding world-forward direction is:

`forwardX = -sin(yaw)`

`forwardZ = cos(yaw)`

The yaw derived from actual horizontal velocity must therefore use:

`yaw = atan2(-velocityX, velocityZ)`

Use this same convention consistently across:

- Model construction.
- Autonomous movement.
- Player-controlled movement.
- Camera positioning.
- Turning.
- Directional animation.
- Shadows.
- Target tracking.
- Species embodiment.

Do not solve the problem by randomly adding 180 degrees to selected camera calculations.

Audit and correct the underlying transformation system.

An animal must face the direction it is actually travelling.

